Title:
Don’t Be So Primitive: Evolving the Module Stomping Staging Chain (Using Undocumented Kernel Transitions to Achieve Single-Primitive Module Stomping)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post describes an evolution of Windows module stomping that collapses the typical multi-API staging chain into a single user-mode primitive by relying on `WriteProcessMemory` (WPM) to perform implicit page-protection transitions in the kernel.  
- Instead of loading a “sacrificial” signed DLL via `LoadLibrary` (which creates noisy module-load telemetry), it advocates profiling target applications to find naturally loaded modules with dormant, stable code caves in file-backed `.text` sections suitable for overwriting.  
- The key idea is that WPM → `NtWriteVirtualMemory` can temporarily make RX pages writable internally and then restore protections, eliminating explicit `VirtualProtect(Ex)` calls and breaking common user-mode API-sequence detections.  
- A PoC demonstrates injecting a stageless Cobalt Strike HTTPS beacon into `explorer.exe` by stomping an offset inside `msi.dll` using a “remote-stomp” utility.  
- The author emphasizes this is sequence-evasion (reducing user-mode artifacts), not true stealth: kernel/ETW telemetry, driver callbacks, and memory integrity scans can still detect text-section modification and protection flips.  
- Useful for red team operators, implant/loader developers, and defenders modeling detection beyond fragile user-mode hook chains.

Technical Focus:
- Targeted module stomping in naturally loaded DLLs (file-backed `.text` modification)
- Staging-chain reduction to a single primitive (`WriteProcessMemory`)
- Kernel transitions behind WPM (`NtWriteVirtualMemory`, implicit protection changes / `MiProtectVirtualMemory`)
- Profiling/hunting for stable dormant code caves in real applications
- Execution handoff methods (thread creation/hijack, callback manipulation)
- Detection considerations (ETW, kernel callbacks, memory-vs-disk integrity checks)

Use Cases:
- Reducing user-mode API telemetry for process injection tradecraft
- Implant staging inside common enterprise processes without anomalous module loads
- Building/benchmarking module-cave profiling pipelines for “safe” stomp targets
- Developing detections for text-section tampering and kernel-level protection flips
- Testing EDR resilience against single-primitive injection patterns

Keywords:
module stomping, targeted module stomping, process injection, WriteProcessMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory, VirtualProtectEx, LoadLibrary, PEB walking, InLoadOrderModuleList, RX to RW transition, MiProtectVirtualMemory, ETW, kernel callbacks, file-backed memory, .text section, code cave, explorer.exe, msi.dll, Cobalt Strike beacon, memory integrity scanning